<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font
color="#0000ff"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
size="4"><strong>HUMOR</strong></font></font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">A first grade class
teacher, for the &quot;Natural World&quot; section of her class, had a
lesson about owls.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">She explained to the
class about how they are nocturnal, how they can swivel their heads so far
in either direction that it looks like they can turn them completely
around, etc. Then she described how the birds swoop down to get their prey
for dinner.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Following the lesson,
she asked the children to draw a picture of one of the fascinating things
owls do.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">Looking over the
pictures, she praised and applauded each child on their efforts. She was
puzzled, however, by one drawing. It was of an owl with its wings neatly
folded, its eyes closed and a dead mouse nearby.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">&quot;Exactly what is
this showing?&quot; she asked.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0.17in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">The little girl
explained, &quot;It's just like you said 'The owl swoops down and prays
before dinner&quot;!</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font
color="#ff0000"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
size="4"><strong>&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt;</strong></font></font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font
color="#0000ff"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
size="4"><strong>THOUGHT PROVOKING &ndash;
INSPIRATIONAL</strong></font></font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><strong><font
face="Arial, sans-serif">Being Fed</font></strong></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">The bulldozer was
plowing through a strip of land between the north and south highway U.S.1
in Delray Beach. Toppled pine trees were piled in a heap, waiting to be
hauled away. The black soil was churning as the blade of the dozer forged
ahead at a slow speed. There were about 50 white birds on the scene,
hunting and pecking in a furious manner. They were so intent on the job at
hand, I was afraid the blade would wipe some of them out ! Their long legs
took them here and fro, as they bent their long necks to the ground. Their
pointed bills moved rapidly along the ground gathering whatever nourishment
was available for them in the soil.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">My husband said the
bulldozer was unearthing a multitude of hidden bugs and grubs, a veritable
feast for these birds. That blade churning the earth was making a hidden
banquet available for this flock of fortunate feathered ones.
</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">There are times when I
need God&rsquo;s Word and His plan to be dug up and placed before me. This
need is filled when I attend church, because I always walk away feeling
full and satisfied. The minister, prayer leader, choir, and hymns are like
the bulldozer. They turn up and bring to the surface all of God&rsquo;s
good nibbles for me to partake in. They unearth many truths of the
scripture using song, sermon and prayer, and I partake of all that has been
made available to me during the service. Just like the birds kept their
beak to the ground, I keep my heart open and close to the source of
nourishment.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">There are many ways to
receive the Word of God, but this is the one way God brought to my mind as
I saw that bulldozer at work amid the flock of hungry
birds.</font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><font face="Arial,
sans-serif"><font size="2">.....Marion Smith (</font></font><font
