<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in"><br />
</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font
color="#ff0000"><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font
size="4"><strong>&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt; &lt;&gt;&lt;
&lt;&gt;&lt;</strong></font></font></font></p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="MARGIN-TOP: 0in; MARGIN-BOTTOM: 0in" align="center"><font
face="Arial, sans-serif"><strong>UNDIE MAN TO THE
RESCUE!</strong></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">Last night my grandson, Cobi, was putting his pajamas on in the
bathroom when suddenly, the door swung open and he jumped out. Wearing his
underwear on his head, he announced, &quot;It&rsquo;s Undie Man to the
rescue!&quot;</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">Cobi loves super heroes. Last week when I bought him new shoes,
his choice did not depend upon color, style, or comfort, but upon which
super hero decorated the side of each shoe. When Cobi doesn&rsquo;t want to
do something, like taking medicine, I suggest that a super hero could do
it, and suddenly his attitude changes. He flexes his muscles, sticks out
his chest, and transforms into a fearless boy who can do
anything.</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">I
could take a lesson from him. I&rsquo;m no hero and I&rsquo;m not super
compared to anybody, but I can live fearlessly by reminding myself of the
super heroes of faith. Their stories in both the Old Testament and the New
Testament of the Bible illustrate how God&rsquo;s people have managed to
live without fear in the past and how I can do it today.</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">The Bible admonishes us to &quot;fear not&quot; and to &quot;be
strong and courageous.&quot; These things are easy to talk about, but
tougher to put into practice, especially when your life makes Job&rsquo;s
look like a vacation in paradise.</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">David was hated and hunted by the King and his armies, yet he
trusted God; and God took care of him.</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">Paul was hated by leaders of the Jewish religion. He was captured,
beaten, and imprisoned over and over, but he remembered that God was with
him. That knowledge helped him get through it all. In a letter to the
Corinthian church, Paul wrote, &quot;This all-surpassing power is from God
and not from us.&quot; (2 Corinthians 4:7)</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">When Jesus walked on the water toward the boat full of his
apostles, Peter jumped out of the boat and headed toward him. (Matthew
14:28-30) He did well until he took his eyes off Jesus and looked around at
the wind and the waves. Then he began to sink. </font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">Peter knew Jesus had the power to help him walk on water, or he
never would have left the boat; but he quickly forgot his goal when he saw
how powerful the forces around him were. He needed a reminder to keep his
eyes on Jesus. When he did that, he was fine and stayed above the
waves.</font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt" size="2">We
know God wants us to triumph in the midst of trouble. We know He&rsquo;s
willing to give us the power to do it. We just forget sometimes because
we&rsquo;re so busy looking at the problems that we take our eyes off the
one who is stretching out His hand to help us. </font></font></p>
<p><font face="Arial, sans-serif"><font style="FONT-SIZE: 11pt"
size="2">When &quot;Undie Man&quot; emerges from my bathroom, I&rsquo;m
reminded to look to God for strength to live out the superhero life
He&rsquo;s called me to. He provides power for me to leap over
insurmountable obstacles that Satan puts in my way. I may not get a cape or
an insignia on my chest, but God gives me everything I need to be
victorious.</font></font></p>
